{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>A viper</b> — it is a name for a serpent that bites the heel (שׁוֹף עָקֵב), and the letter ע is doubled in it. They said in the Jerusalem Talmud, in tractate Terumot (8:3): it is a small species and Shefifon is its name, and it resembles a strand of hair, and authority was given to the earth to split open before it. Now, he compared Samson to a serpent because Samson did not come against his enemies in battle as the judges or the kings do, but he alone would go out against them like a viper that emerges from its hole upon wayfarers, or like the small species among serpents that is not noticed at all by passersby:" "And the sense of <b>that bites the horse's heels</b> is an allusion to the two pillars on which the house rested (Judges 16:29), and the three thousand who were on the roof (ibid. v. 27) — they are the rider who falls backward, for when the serpent bites the horse's heels, the horse rears up its head and its forelegs, and its rider falls backward:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:03.949264559Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}